EXAM FACTS (from the official Exam Guide v1.0, July 2026):
- 60 questions, 120 minutes, pass mark 720/1000, delivered by Pearson VUE
- Multiple-choice and multiple-response (each question says how many answers to pick)
- Non-technical: no coding. It tests practical judgment about using Claude at work
- Questions are workplace scenarios: "What is the BEST / FIRST / MOST appropriate action?"

THE 7 DOMAINS AND WEIGHTS:
1. Prompting and Task Execution (14%): clear instructions, giving context and audience, examples, specifying output format, breaking big tasks into steps, iterating on drafts
2. Output Evaluation and Validation (21%, the biggest): checking accuracy and completeness, spotting hallucinations, inconsistencies and bias, fact-checking, deciding when human review is required, adapting output for the audience, choosing the right output format (inline, artifact/document, structured data)
3. Product and Model Selection (12%): choosing between Claude model tiers (Opus = most capable, Sonnet = balanced, Haiku = fastest/cheapest) and Claude products (Claude apps, Projects, web search/Research, connectors, Claude Code, Cowork, Claude in Chrome/Excel/PowerPoint, plans like Pro/Team/Enterprise)
4. Workflow Integration and Solution Design (16%): identifying good tasks for Claude, designing workflows with human checkpoints, piloting and measuring results
5. Configuration and Knowledge Management (12%): Projects, project knowledge and instructions, user preferences, styles, memory, skills, keeping reference material current, context window limits
6. Governance, Risk, and Responsible Use (15%): data privacy and company policy, human accountability, transparency, bias and fairness, copyright, Anthropic's Usage Policy, admin and security controls, least-privilege access
7. Troubleshooting and Optimization (10%): diagnosing why outputs are poor (missing context, unclear prompt, wrong model, overly long chat, outdated knowledge) and fixing one thing at a time

KEY PRINCIPLES THE EXAM REWARDS:
- The human is accountable; verify before acting on important output
- Match checking effort to the stakes (light check for internal drafts; careful verification plus human review for legal, financial, medical, HR or customer-facing content)
- Prefer the proportionate, practical answer over extreme ones ("always trust" or "never use AI")
- Use the least data and least access needed; follow company policy

